.TH LOGIN 6
.SH NAME
login \- password-authenticated key exchange protocol
.SH DESCRIPTION
The following protocol is used between a client such as
.B login
in
.IR security-login (2),
and a certificate signing process such as
.IR logind (8),
to justify the latter's issuing a certificate that can
later be presented to an Inferno service
to establish credentials.
.PP
A shared secret must previously be agreed between
user and certifying authority (CA).
The protocol proves it each way and agrees a session key with
PAK (Boyko, MacKenzie and Patel; the variant Plan 9's
.IR secstore
uses), into whose proofs and session key an ML-KEM (FIPS 203)
exchange is mixed.
Neither a recorded conversation nor a conversation held with
either side by someone without the password lets a guess at the
password be checked off line: each run tests at most one guess, and
the CA's
.IR logind (8)
logs the failures.
A recording stays sealed unless both the discrete logarithm and
ML-KEM are broken.
.PP
In the description below:
.TF kem
.PD
.TP
.I kem
is
.B mlkem768
or, for a client in CNSA mode (\f5/env/cnsamode\fP set),
.BR mlkem1024 .
A CA in CNSA mode refuses
.BR mlkem768 ,
saying so.
.TP
.I p, q, g
are RFC 5114's 2048-bit group with a 256-bit subgroup of order
.IR q ,
generated by
.IR g .
.TP
.I pw
is the 32-byte SHA-256 of the password, as
.IR changelogin (8)
keeps it.
.TP
.I H
is the password's element of the subgroup:
SHA-384 over a counter byte (0 to 5) and the fields
.BR inflogin2 ,
.BR password ,
.I name
and
.IR pw ,
the six digests read as one big-endian number, reduced mod
.I p
and raised to
.RI ( p -1)/ q .
.TP
.I x, y
are random exponents mod
.IR q .
.TP
.I m
is
.IR g ** x ·H
mod
.IR p ;
.I mu
is
.IR g ** y
mod
.IR p .
Each side refuses a value that is not in the subgroup or is 1.
.TP
.I ek, ct, ss
are the client's ML-KEM encapsulation key, the CA's
ciphertext to it, and their shared secret.
.TP
.I sigma
is
.IR mu ** x
=
.RI ( m ·H** -1)** y
mod
.IR p .
.TP
.I h(label)
is SHA-384 of
.I label
followed by the transcript: the fields
.BR inflogin2 ,
.IR kem ,
.IR name ,
.IR m ,
.IR mu ,
.IR ek ,
.IR ct ,
.IR sigma ,
.I ss
and
.IR H ,
each preceded by its length in four bytes, big-endian.
Numbers are in base 64, as
.B IPint.iptob64
writes them.
.PP
The protocol follows.  ``user→CA xxx'' means that the user
sends the message ``xxx'' to the certifying authority.
Any party can send an error instead of a message at any
point to terminate the protocol.
.IP
.EX
user→CA	inflogin2 \fIkem\fP
CA→user	inflogin2
.sp 1v
user→CA	\fIname\fP, \fIm\fP, \fIek\fP
CA→user	\fImu\fP, \fIct\fP, \fIh\fP(server)
.sp 1v
user→CA	\fIh\fP(client)
CA→user	ok
.sp 1v
CA→user	CA's public key
user→CA	user's public key
CA→user	user's public key certificate, alpha, p
.EE
.PP
Each side checks the other's proof before it sends anything that
depends on its own:
the user checks
.IR h (server)
before sending
.IR h (client),
and the CA sends
.B ok
only when
.IR h (client)
is right.
Then each pushes
.IR ssl (3)
onto the connection with
.B aes_256_gcm
under its own secret each way, made from
.IR h (session)
by
.B secrets
in
.IR security-ssl (2),
and the last three messages go sealed;
.I alpha
and
.I p
are the Diffie-Hellman constants the user's
.B Authinfo
carries for
.IR keyring-auth (2).
.PP
A name with no entry is answered as one with an entry: the CA
runs the exchange with an element of nobody's, and the client is told
only that the name or secret is incorrect.
An entry that has expired is reported as such once the password is
proved.
.PP
A CA answers a client of the protocol before this one (Lucent's
encrypted key exchange) with an error, and a client told by a CA
that it speaks only that protocol says so and goes no further.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/loginpak.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR security-intro (2),
.IR security-login (2),
